        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H1",
            "content": "Mathematical formulation of the Multiplexer",
            "scrollToId": 0
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H3",
            "content": "An MUX of order $p$, $M(p)$ is formulated as:"
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H3",
            "content": "$ \\begin{equation}F(\\underbrace{x1,\\ldots,xp}\\{\\text{inputs}}, \\underbrace{y0,\\ldots,yk}\\{\\text{control}})= \\sum\\{i=0}^{2^p-1}\\underbrace{\\left(\\dot{x1}\\dot{x2}\\cdots\\dot{xp}\\right)i}\\{i^{th}\\text{ Miniterm of }F}\\cdot y_i \\end{equation}$"
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H3",
            "content": "where $k=2^p-1$ and $\\dot{xj} \\in \\\\{xj, \\overline{x_j}\\\\}$"
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H3",
            "content": "More examples can be found in [1] (Section on Data Selectors)."
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H1",
            "content": "Shannon theorem with MUX",
            "scrollToId": 1
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H3",
            "content": "Shannon decomposition theorem can also be implemented using MUXs:"
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H3",
            "content": "$ f(x1,x2,x3,\\ldots, xn) = x1 \\cdot f1(1,x2,x3,\\ldots,xn) + \\overline{x1} \\cdot f0(0,x2,x3,\\ldots,xn) $"
        },
        {
            "type": "widget",
            "sub_type": "image",
            "content": {
                "link": "/assets/images/comb/mux-shannon.svg",
                "description": "MUX based implementation of Shannon decomposition theorem."
            }
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H3",
            "content": "See [2] for a more general description."
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H1",
            "content": "Interactive MUX based logic gates",
            "scrollToId": 2
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H3",
            "content": "The set $\\\\{M(1),0,1\\\\}$ is functionally complete (See section Functionally Complete \\*Operation Sets in [3] or Wikipedia:Functional completeness (https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Functional_completeness&oldid=986190082)). Therefore, any logic function can be implemented using multiplexers, check the interactive circuit below, which implements the basic logic gates using MUXs:"
        },
        {
            "type": "widget",
            "sub_type": "bullet_list",
            "items": [
                "MUX-based AND gate",
                "MUX-based OR gate",
                "MUX-based NAND gate",
                "MUX-based NOR gate",
                "MUX-based NOT gate"
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "widget",
            "sub_type": "image",
            "content": {
                "link": "https://circuitverse.org/simulator/embed/mux-based-not-gate"
            }
        },
